# rules: java-securerandom-told-what-to-produce
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-338.java
# from: CWE-338 (Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG), CWE-330, CWE-336
#
# SecureRandom is the right class, and seeding it by hand undoes the reason
# for reaching for it. With no argument it seeds itself from the operating
# system. Given a byte array, or told `setSeed` before it has produced
# anything, it produces exactly the sequence that seed determines - so a
# constant in the source means every installation generates the same session
# identifiers, the same reset tokens and the same nonces, in the same order,
# for as long as the program exists.
#
# The clock counts as a constant here. `System.currentTimeMillis()` is a
# number an attacker can bracket to a second or two, which is a few thousand
# candidate sequences, which is not a search.
#
# `new byte[16]` is in the guard because an uninitialised Java array is
# sixteen zeroes and not a seed. It is easy to read as "sixteen random bytes"
# and it is the shape that gets written when someone means to fill it and does
# not.
#
# Deliberately over-reported: `setSeed` *after* the generator has produced
# output is defined to supplement its state rather than replace it, and is
# harmless. Nothing in the syntax says which came first, so both are reported.
# That direction costs a false positive rather than a predictable key, and the
# fix for the harmless case is to delete a line that was doing nothing.
#
# java-weak-random already reports `java.util.Random`, so Random is not named
# here; two ids on one line is worse than one.

["new SecureRandom($SEED)", "$SR.setSeed($SEED)"] as $seeded
| "^\"|^[0-9]|^'|^new byte\\[|^\\{|currentTimeMillis\\(\\)|nanoTime\\(\\)|getTime\\(\\)|hashCode\\(\\)" as $determined
| scan_ast("*.java"; $seeded)
| where_capture("SEED"; $determined)
| focus("SEED")
| finding("java-securerandom-told-what-to-produce";
    "this decides what the generator produces, so every run of the program "
    + "produces the same tokens in the same order")
| report
